<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Serbian Patriarchate of Peć</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1346">1346</a>–<a href="/years/1766">1766</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Serbian Patriarchate of Peć was the patriarchate of the Serbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a>, seated at the Patriarchal Monastery of Peć in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>. The Serbian archbishopric was raised to a patriarchate in 1346, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a> was crowned emperor; after lapsing under Ottoman rule it was restored in 1557 with the support of the grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> Mehmed Sokolović, and abolished again in 1766. It sustained Serbian church life under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/serbian-patriarchate-of-pec.jpg" alt="Serbian Patriarchate of Peć" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vladimir Vukotic · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manastir_Pecka_Patrijarsija_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
